+/// Boundary novelty: how far a clue's resegmentation departs from the
+/// target's *own* word boundaries.
+///
+/// The clue's job is to re-cut the target, so the question this axis asks
+/// is not "how much do these two offset sets have in common" — it is
+/// "has this clue resegmented the target at all".  A Jaccard distance
+/// answers the first question, and it answers it in a way that charges a
+/// clue for the target's spelling: its denominator is the union of the two
+/// boundary sets, so a clue pays for a boundary it reproduces out of a
+/// total that grows with the number of boundaries the clue itself invents.
+/// The result is that the price of one reproduced boundary depends on how
+/// long the clue is, the axis cannot tell a clue that has resegmented
+/// *completely* from one that has resegmented *halfway*, and — because the
+/// visible candidates of a real target are overwhelmingly the completely
+/// resegmented ones — the axis is a step function whose only surviving
+/// information is the amount of the target's own segmentation a clue
+/// failed to discard.
+///
+/// So the charge is the *excess* of the reproduced share over the half a
+/// resegmentation is entitled to keep:
+///
+/// ```text
+/// charge = max(0, 2 * kept / total - 1)
+/// ```
+///
+/// A clue that keeps at most half of the target's word boundaries has
+/// resegmented and pays nothing, however many new boundaries it invented
+/// and however many of the target's it merged away; a clue that keeps
+/// more than half has mostly reproduced the target's own segmentation, and
+/// pays in proportion.  Reproducing the target's segmentation exactly is
+/// charged in full, so the axis still refuses to call the target a clue.
 ///
-/// Counting only the boundaries that disappeared would give zero novelty
-/// to a useful split that preserved an original boundary, so the measure
-/// is a Jaccard distance and rewards both added and removed boundaries.
+/// Both facts the measure needs are counts, so it does not depend on the
+/// clue's length at all: the same divergence costs the same whether the
+/// clue is three words or seven.  A target with no inner word boundary has
+/// nothing in dispute, so nothing can be charged.
+fn boundary_novelty_from_counts(kept: usize, total: usize) -> f64 {
+    if total == 0 {
+        return 1.0;
+    }
+    let retained = kept as f64 / total as f64;
+    (2.0 - 2.0 * retained).clamp(0.0, 1.0)
